# Migrating `TimedExecutor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The cooperative cancellation model via `CancellationToken` is the replacement.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerException is not null)
        {
            // Unwrap single inner exceptions for consistent behavior
            throw ex.InnerException;
        }
    }

    // Async-native version (preferred)
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Non-cooperative work** | If `work` ignores the token (e.g., blocking I/O), it won't stop on timeout — it just becomes orphaned. There's no safe forced-kill equivalent. |
| **`Task.Run` vs `Thread`** | Uses thread pool threads; avoid for very long-running CPU work unless you pass `TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning`. |
| **`WaitAsync` (.NET 6+)** | Returns immediately on cancellation even if the underlying task keeps running — useful but can leak resources. |
| **Exception unwrapping** | `Task.Run` wraps exceptions in `AggregateException`; `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` auto-unwraps, but nested tasks may not. |
| **`default(T)` race condition** | The original code had a race: if `thread.Join` returned `true` but the closure hadn't assigned `result` yet. Task-based version eliminates this. |

## If You Truly Need Forced Termination

For untrusted/uncooperative code, the only safe .NET 8 option is **process isolation**:

```csharp
// Run work in a child process and kill the process on timeout
var process = Process.Start("worker.exe", args);
if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException("...");
}
```

## Caller Migration

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework)
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(() => ComputeExpensiveResult(), TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

// After (.NET 8) — caller must honor the token
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(
    ct => ComputeExpensiveResult(ct),
    TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

The fundamental shift: .NET 8 uses **cooperative cancellation** — work must periodically check `ct.IsCancellationRequested` or pass the token to I/O APIs. There is no equivalent to the old "rip the thread out from under you" behavior.
